# main pgfexamples/inference/loopy/hard_distributions/d_finite/polya_urn.ppy
# Pólya urn: start with one red and one black ball; each draw returns the ball together with a new
# ball of the same colour. After n draws, red - 1 is uniform on {0, ..., n} (Beta-binomial with
# α = β = 1; Johnson, Kemp and Kotz, "Univariate Discrete Distributions", 3rd ed.).
#
# Here the number of draws is geometric(1/2), Pr[n] = 2^-(n+1), so
#   Pr[red - 1 = k] = Σ_{n ≥ k} 2^-(n+1)/(n+1) = ln 2 - Σ_{m=1}^{k} 2^-m/m,
# with generating function ln(2 - x)/(1 - x) in x = red - 1: transcendental (logarithmic) but
# D-finite. The tail is about 2^-k/k, i.e. light-tailed; yule_simon_urn.ppy draws until the first
# black ball instead, which is heavy-tailed. Class: D-finite PGF, AST, all moments finite.
#
# The choice probability red/(red + black) depends on program variables; Prodigy computes the
# choice by a case split over the finitely many values of red and black in each unrolled
# iteration. polya_urn_coins.ppy draws the ball with fair coins only.
#
# What Prodigy proves: with "Unroll" (20 iterations) Pr[red == 1] ∈ [0.693147, 0.693148], which
# contains ln 2, and Pr[red == 2] ∈ [0.193147, 0.193148]. This takes about 1 s on GiNaC and 15 s on
# SymPy; SymEngine is much slower (issue #224). Strategies that reason about the whole loop give no
# result: red and black take infinitely many values there.

draws: nat;
red: nat;
black: nat;

draws = geometric(1/2);
red = 1;
black = 1;
while (draws > 0) {
    {red = red + 1;} [red/(red+black)] {black = black + 1;}
    draws = draws - 1;
}
query Pr[red == 1];  # expected: ln 2 ≈ 0.6931
query Pr[red == 2];  # expected: ln 2 - 1/2 ≈ 0.1931
query Ex[red];  # expected: 3/2
query Ex[black];  # expected: 3/2
